    "question": {
      ":question/id": "22717ed8-2972-5523-855a-3f86361f9215",
      ":question/problem": "Express\n\n$$\n5\\cos^{2}\\left(\\frac{6\\pi}{11}\\right)-5\\sin^{2}\\left(\\frac{6\\pi}{11}\\right)\n$$\n\nas a single trigonometric ratio.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Factor out the common coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&5\\cos^{2}\\left(\\frac{6\\pi}{11}\\right)-5\\sin^{2}\\left(\\frac{6\\pi}{11}\\right) \\\\\n&=5\\left(\\cos^{2}\\left(\\frac{6\\pi}{11}\\right)-\\sin^{2}\\left(\\frac{6\\pi}{11}\\right)\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall the **double-angle formula for cosine**:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x-\\sin^2 x=\\cos(2x).\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=\\frac{6\\pi}{11}$ and double the angle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&5\\left(\\cos^{2}\\left(\\frac{6\\pi}{11}\\right)-\\sin^{2}\\left(\\frac{6\\pi}{11}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&=5\\cos\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{6\\pi}{11}\\right) \\\\\n&=5\\cos\\left(\\frac{12\\pi}{11}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly the angle doubles; the common coefficient stays outside the cosine.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
